from (values
('127.690.d.4.G',4,'A persona usually includes goals, needs and:','["a medical record", "frustrations", "a social security number", "a bank balance"]'::jsonb,1,'Pain points guide design.','original'),
('127.690.d.4.H',3,'A research report should present interview and observation data using:','["jargon only", "blank slides", "clear summaries and visuals", "raw unorganized notes only"]'::jsonb,2,'Summaries make findings usable.','original'),
('127.690.d.4.H',4,'After reporting findings, a designer should end the presentation with:','["unrelated opinions", "a blank slide", "jokes only", "recommendations based on the data"]'::jsonb,3,'Insights should lead to action.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.A',3,'Balance, contrast and alignment are examples of:','["design principles", "server settings", "file types", "coding bugs"]'::jsonb,0,'They guide how elements are arranged.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.A',4,'Using large text for headings and small text for details helps show:','["random layout", "visual hierarchy", "hidden menus", "broken links"]'::jsonb,1,'Hierarchy ranks importance.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.B',3,'A visceral reaction to a design is a person''s:','["technical audit", "slow, detailed analysis", "immediate, gut-level response to how it looks and feels", "written report"]'::jsonb,2,'Visceral responses are instant.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.B',4,'A clean, attractive welcome screen that makes users feel good in the first seconds builds:','["slower loading", "less trust", "more confusion", "a positive first impression"]'::jsonb,3,'First impressions affect experience.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.C',3,'Red is often used for warnings and errors because people tend to associate it with:','["danger or urgency", "coldness and ice", "calm and sleep", "nature and growth"]'::jsonb,0,'Color carries meaning.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.C',4,'A meditation app would likely choose soft blues and greens to encourage:','["aggression", "a calm mood", "alarm", "urgency and panic"]'::jsonb,1,'Color influences emotion.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.D',3,'Many readers scan a web page in an F-shaped pattern, so designers put key information:','["in tiny text", "in hidden menus", "near the top and left", "at the very bottom"]'::jsonb,2,'Scanning patterns guide layout.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.D',4,'Because people scan rather than read every word, designers use clear headings and short:','["tiny fonts", "dense tables only", "unbroken walls of text", "paragraphs and bullet lists"]'::jsonb,3,'Scannable content helps users.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.E',3,'Hick''s Law says that the more choices a person has, the longer it takes them to:','["make a decision", "walk", "blink", "sleep"]'::jsonb,0,'More options slow decisions.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.E',4,'Weber''s Law states that people notice a change based on its size relative to the original. So a tiny change to a very large price:','["is illegal", "may go unnoticed", "is always obvious", "is always rejected"]'::jsonb,1,'Noticeability is proportional.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.F',3,'After a while, you stop noticing a steady background hum. This is called:','["a bug", "a network error", "sensory adaptation", "hearing loss"]'::jsonb,2,'Senses adapt to constant stimuli.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.F',4,'A perceptual set is the tendency to perceive things based on:','["file size", "font size", "screen size", "expectations and past experience"]'::jsonb,3,'Expectations shape perception.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.G',3,'In human information processing, taking in information through the eyes and ears is the stage of:','["sensing", "deciding", "forgetting", "responding"]'::jsonb,0,'Sensing is the first stage.','original'),
('127.690.d.5.G',4,'After sensing, the brain interprets what was sensed in the stage called:','["sleeping", "perceiving", "printing", "charging"]'::jsonb,1,'Perceiving interprets sensory input.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.A',3,'Watching how nurses actually use a patient-record screen on a busy ward helps identify:','["office furniture", "new logo ideas", "real-world problems and needs", "computer brands"]'::jsonb,2,'Context reveals true needs.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.A',4,'A designer asks bus riders why they skip the trip-planning app and learns they have no signal underground. The need identified is:','["a brighter screen", "more ads", "smaller buttons", "reliable use without internet"]'::jsonb,3,'Needs emerge from real conditions.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.B',3,'The accessibility principle ''perceivable'' means users must be able to:','["sense the content, for example by seeing or hearing it", "print it", "steal it", "buy it"]'::jsonb,0,'Content must be presentable to senses.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.B',4,'Making sure a site can be used with only a keyboard supports which accessibility principle?','["profitable", "operable", "perceivable", "portable"]'::jsonb,1,'Operable means usable with various controls.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.C',3,'UX design focuses on the whole experience, while visual design focuses mainly on:','["the network", "the database", "the look and style of the interface", "the budget"]'::jsonb,2,'Visual design is the appearance.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.C',4,'Interaction design, a part of UX, deals with:','["only color choices", "only server speed", "only printing", "how users and a product respond to each other"]'::jsonb,3,'Interaction design shapes behavior flows.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.D',3,'A designer presents a user''s problem as a short story about a person''s day to help the team:','["feel empathy and understand the need", "avoid prototypes", "skip research", "hide the user"]'::jsonb,0,'Stories make needs vivid.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.D',4,'Storytelling in a design pitch works best when it links the user''s problem to:','["random facts", "the proposed solution", "the company''s party", "the designer''s lunch"]'::jsonb,1,'A story should lead to the idea.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.E',3,'A wireframe is a simple layout that shows:','["user salaries", "final colors and photos", "where content and controls will go", "finished code"]'::jsonb,2,'Wireframes outline structure.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.E',4,'A prototype differs from a wireframe in that a prototype can:','["only show text", "only show boxes", "only be printed", "simulate how a user would move through the design"]'::jsonb,3,'Prototypes are interactive.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.F',3,'A structured usability test uses the same set of tasks for each participant so results can be:','["compared fairly", "hidden", "guessed", "ignored"]'::jsonb,0,'Consistency enables comparison.','original'),
('127.690.d.6.F',4,'After a test shows that users miss a button, the designer makes a change and tests again. This is called:','["deletion", "iteration", "abandonment", "plagiarism"]'::jsonb,1,'Iterating improves the design.','original'),
('127.690.d.7.A',3,'UX research feeds into design thinking by providing:','["extra file formats", "random opinions", "evidence about what users need", "decorations"]'::jsonb,2,'Research informs each stage.','original'),
('127.690.d.7.A',4,'The ''define'' stage of design thinking uses research to state:','["the price", "the schedule", "the slogan", "the problem to solve"]'::jsonb,3,'Research findings define the problem.','original'),
('127.690.d.7.B',3,'A project manager uses a method like Agile in UX projects to work in:','["short cycles with regular feedback", "secret phases", "one giant step", "random order"]'::jsonb,0,'Agile uses sprints.','original'),
('127.690.d.7.B',4,'In many UX project methods, stages such as research, design and testing are handled by team members with different:','["desk colors", "roles", "phones", "passwords"]'::jsonb,1,'Roles divide responsibility.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.A',3,'Typeface, color, shape, texture, space and form are all:','["server types", "legal terms", "design elements", "HTML tags"]'::jsonb,2,'They are building blocks of visual design.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.A',4,'Choosing a rounded, friendly style of lettering for a kids'' app uses the design element of:','["texture only", "audio", "video", "typeface"]'::jsonb,3,'Fonts set tone.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.B',3,'A page where headings, images and text feel like they belong together shows the principle of:','["unity", "randomness", "chaos", "clutter"]'::jsonb,0,'Unity makes elements cohere.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.B',4,'Making the most important button larger than the others uses the principle of:','["noise", "scale and hierarchy", "delay", "speed"]'::jsonb,1,'Size signals importance.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.C',3,'In HTML, the element used to create a hyperlink is:','["<p>", "<br>", "<a>", "<h1>"]'::jsonb,2,'The anchor tag makes links.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.C',4,'The HTML element used for the largest heading on a page is:','["<img>", "<ul>", "<a>", "<h1>"]'::jsonb,3,'h1 is the top-level heading.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.E',3,'Quick, rough sketches of an interface help a designer to:','["explore ideas fast before using software", "pay taxes", "finalize the code", "install updates"]'::jsonb,0,'Sketching is cheap exploration.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.E',4,'A designer sketches five different layouts for a home screen to:','["delay the project", "compare options before choosing one", "avoid feedback", "use more paper for no reason"]'::jsonb,1,'Variety supports choice.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.F',3,'Design software used to create wireframes lets a designer drag and place:','["tax forms", "real servers", "boxes, text and buttons on a canvas", "physical bricks"]'::jsonb,2,'Tools place interface elements.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.F',4,'Reusable components in wireframing software, like a navigation bar, help keep screens:','["incomplete", "random", "slower to change", "consistent and quicker to build"]'::jsonb,3,'Components save time.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.G',3,'Design fidelity describes how close a design is to the:','["final product", "office layout", "budget", "first idea in someone''s head"]'::jsonb,0,'Fidelity measures realism.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.G',4,'Moving from sketch to wireframe to prototype to visuals gradually:','["removes all detail", "adds detail and realism", "deletes the project", "hides the design"]'::jsonb,1,'Fidelity increases step by step.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.H',3,'Digital assets for a website include images, icons and:','["office keys", "parking passes", "fonts and logos", "coffee cups"]'::jsonb,2,'Assets are files used in the design.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.H',4,'Exporting an icon in several sizes for different screens is part of:','["repairing hardware", "painting a wall", "writing a contract", "producing digital assets"]'::jsonb,3,'Assets must fit varied devices.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.i',3,'Browser-based interfaces must work across different:','["screen sizes and browsers", "paper sizes", "power outlets", "car models"]'::jsonb,0,'Responsive design handles variety.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.i',4,'A mobile interface usually puts navigation where a user''s thumb can reach it because:','["thumbs are unused", "phones are often held and used with one hand", "phones have no screens", "keyboards are required"]'::jsonb,1,'Ergonomics guide mobile layout.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.ii',3,'A vector graphic is made from:','["only text files", "only pixels", "mathematical lines and shapes that scale cleanly", "only sounds"]'::jsonb,2,'Vectors scale without blur.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.ii',4,'A photograph is best stored as a raster image because it:','["is drawn only with lines", "is a math formula", "has no color", "contains detailed color changes in pixels"]'::jsonb,3,'Rasters capture photographic detail.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.iii',3,'The front end of a website is the part that:','["users see and interact with", "only the programmer sees", "stays hidden in the database", "only the server uses"]'::jsonb,0,'Front end is the interface.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.iii',4,'The back end of a website handles things like:','["painting buttons", "storing data and running server logic", "drawing icons", "choosing fonts"]'::jsonb,1,'Back end works behind the scenes.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.iv',3,'A basic web page with links and graphics needs HTML for structure and an image tag to:','["print the page", "play a game", "display the graphic", "send an email"]'::jsonb,2,'Images are embedded with markup.','original'),
('127.690.d.8.D.iv',4,'To make a graphic on a page open another page when clicked, the designer wraps the image in a:','["table", "comment", "heading", "link (anchor element)"]'::jsonb,3,'Linked images use anchors.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
insert into public.question_bank (teks_code, teks_item_id, subject, grade_num, course, difficulty, qtype, prompt, choices, answer_idx, explanation, source, status, gen_batch)
select distinct on (v.prompt) v.code, i.id, d.subject,
       case when i.grade_level = 'K' then 0 when i.grade_level ~ '^[0-9]+' then substring(i.grade_level from '^[0-9]+')::smallint end,
       case when i.grade_level is null then i.grade_course_title end,
       v.diff, 'mc', v.prompt, v.choices, v.ans, v.expl, v.src, 'checked', 'cte_88_v1'
from (values
('127.691.d.2',3,'A designer applies the idea of empathy from class by interviewing real commuters about a transit app. This connects:','["a conceptual foundation to a real-world project", "a bug to a feature", "a font to a color", "a price to a logo"]'::jsonb,0,'Foundations are applied to real users.','original'),
('127.691.d.2',4,'A student connects the principle of consistency to a real redesign by making sure buttons:','["change style on every page", "look and behave the same on every screen", "hide on some screens", "use random labels"]'::jsonb,1,'Consistency is a foundation applied in practice.','original'),
('127.691.d.1.A',3,'A client''s website uses a famous brand''s logo without permission. The designer should identify this as an:','["color choice", "layout pattern", "intellectual property violation", "accessibility feature"]'::jsonb,2,'Unlicensed logos violate rights.','original'),
('127.691.d.1.A',4,'A teammate suggests copying a competitor''s unique icon set exactly. A designer recognizes the risk of:','["stronger contrast", "better branding", "faster loading", "copyright infringement"]'::jsonb,3,'Copying protected art is infringement.','original'),
('127.691.d.1.B',3,'A designer explains to a client the ramifications of using unlicensed photos, such as:','["legal costs and damage to reputation", "faster pages", "higher traffic", "better colors"]'::jsonb,0,'Violations carry consequences.','original'),
('127.691.d.1.B',4,'A designer may communicate ramifications of an IP violation visually, orally or in writing, but the message should always be:','["misleading", "clear and accurate", "vague", "secret"]'::jsonb,1,'Accurate communication guides decisions.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.A',3,'The stages of the UX design process include research, identifying problems, ideating, prototyping and:','["printing", "guessing", "testing", "hiding"]'::jsonb,2,'Testing follows prototyping.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.A',4,'The stage in which a designer defines the specific problem to solve comes right after:','["launch", "billing", "maintenance", "research"]'::jsonb,3,'Research informs problem definition.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.B',3,'Testing a half-finished prototype early lets a designer:','["find problems before spending more time building", "avoid users", "delay all work", "skip design"]'::jsonb,0,'Early tests save effort.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.B',4,'Results from a partial-product test showing users get lost in the menu should lead the designer to:','["finish the code first", "revise the navigation", "ignore the test", "remove the testers"]'::jsonb,1,'Findings drive revisions.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.C',3,'A content strategy plans what content a site needs and:','["the paper stock", "the length of the sales team", "how it will be created and organized", "the keyboard type"]'::jsonb,2,'Strategy covers content planning.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.C',4,'Getting feedback from stakeholders and users on a conceptual design helps a team to:','["skip research", "remove the design", "avoid all decisions", "confirm direction before building details"]'::jsonb,3,'Early feedback reduces rework.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.D',3,'Breaking a design project into tasks with due dates is an example of:','["time management and planning", "image compression", "font selection", "server setup"]'::jsonb,0,'Planning keeps projects on schedule.','original'),
('127.691.d.3.D',4,'A designer finds that user testing will take longer than planned and tells the team early so they can:','["hide the delay", "adjust the schedule", "miss the deadline silently", "cancel testing"]'::jsonb,1,'Early communication helps rescheduling.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.A',3,'A questionnaire template saves time because it can be:','["used once only", "thrown away", "reused for similar research studies", "printed on posters"]'::jsonb,2,'Templates make research efficient.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.A',4,'A summary report template should include sections for goals, method, findings and:','["lyrics", "recipes", "jokes", "recommendations"]'::jsonb,3,'Reports end with next steps.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.B',3,'A project summary that includes insights from the data should connect each conclusion to:','["evidence from the research", "a personal hunch", "a coin flip", "a rumor"]'::jsonb,0,'Conclusions need evidence.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.B',4,'If survey scores and interview comments both point to confusing checkout steps, the designer''s summary should highlight:','["only the best comment", "that consistent finding", "only the worst score", "nothing"]'::jsonb,1,'Converging evidence is strong.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.C',3,'Qualitative research methods such as user interviews and field studies collect:','["only server logs", "only prices", "detailed descriptions of behavior and opinions", "only counts"]'::jsonb,2,'Qualitative = rich description.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.C',4,'A diary study, where users record their daily experiences with a product, is a:','["hardware test", "legal review", "quantitative method", "qualitative method"]'::jsonb,3,'Diary studies yield descriptive data.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.D',3,'A/B testing compares two versions of a page to see which:','["performs better with real users", "has more pages", "costs less to print", "is longer"]'::jsonb,0,'A/B tests compare variants.','original'),
('127.691.d.4.D',4,'A heat map of clicks on a page shows:','["how long the code is", "where users click most often", "who wrote the page", "where the server is"]'::jsonb,1,'Heat maps visualize interaction.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.A',3,'Users keep abandoning their carts at the shipping-cost page. The UX problem is:','["a wrong font", "a slow printer", "unexpected costs appearing late in checkout", "too many photos"]'::jsonb,2,'Problems are specific and observable.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.A',4,'After naming a UX problem, the next step is to list:','["new logos", "email addresses", "staff birthdays", "possible solutions"]'::jsonb,3,'Solutions follow problem identification.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.B',3,'To evaluate possible solutions, a designer compares them against user needs, cost and:','["feasibility", "font size only", "brand slogans", "paper weight"]'::jsonb,0,'Feasibility affects choices.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.B',4,'An action plan for a chosen solution lists tasks, owners and:','["wallpapers", "deadlines", "lunch menus", "parking spots"]'::jsonb,1,'Plans assign time and responsibility.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.C',3,'A presentable content strategy might include a site map and a plan for:','["employee schedules", "office decor", "what content goes on each page", "hardware pricing"]'::jsonb,2,'Strategy organizes content.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.C',4,'Symbolic designs such as icons and logos should be tested to see whether users:','["can sell them", "can print them", "like their color only", "understand what they mean"]'::jsonb,3,'Symbols must be recognizable.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.D',3,'Brainstorming with no judging of ideas at first is a method called:','["unstructured ideation", "final review", "budget approval", "code review"]'::jsonb,0,'Open discussion generates many ideas.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.D',4,'A structured ideation method where each person sketches six quick ideas in a short time is called:','["a marathon", "a rapid sketching session", "a data entry", "an audit"]'::jsonb,1,'Rapid sketches produce variety fast.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.E',3,'A people-centered rationale for choosing an idea explains how the idea:','["looks trendy", "matches the CEO''s taste only", "meets users'' needs", "is cheapest for the designer"]'::jsonb,2,'Rationales tie ideas to users.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.E',4,'Selecting ideas for prototyping should include input from users or team members to:','["delay decisions", "hide ideas", "reduce testing", "avoid personal bias"]'::jsonb,3,'Group input balances bias.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.F',3,'A low-fidelity prototype such as a paper sketch is useful because it is:','["quick and cheap to change", "expensive", "permanent", "fully coded"]'::jsonb,0,'Low-fi allows fast iteration.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.F',4,'A click-through prototype made of images linked together lets testers:','["write final code", "tap through a simulated flow", "hire staff", "print business cards"]'::jsonb,1,'Click-throughs simulate navigation.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.G',3,'A high-fidelity prototype looks and behaves:','["like a blank page", "like a paper note", "very close to the final product", "like a rough doodle"]'::jsonb,2,'High-fi feels real.','original'),
('127.691.d.5.G',4,'A physical prototype of a smart-watch case, 3D printed to test fit, is a:','["wireframe in software", "budget form", "list of tasks", "high-fidelity physical mockup"]'::jsonb,3,'Physical models test form.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.A',3,'Page layouts, content blocks, visual design and navigation must work together to create:','["a coherent user experience", "extra ads", "a slower server", "a random page"]'::jsonb,0,'Elements connect into one experience.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.A',4,'If navigation labels do not match the headings on the pages they open, the connected experience becomes:','["faster", "confusing for users", "clearer", "more secure"]'::jsonb,1,'Mismatches break flow.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.B',3,'Designers must distinguish channels such as web, mobile app and email because each:','["needs no design", "has the same rules", "has different formats and user expectations", "has no users"]'::jsonb,2,'Channels vary.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.B',4,'A message designed for a wide desktop page may need to be reformatted for a narrow:','["router", "fax", "printer", "phone screen"]'::jsonb,3,'Format must fit the channel.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.C',3,'A co-creation workshop invites users to:','["help design the solution with the team", "stay away", "watch only", "approve the final paper"]'::jsonb,0,'Co-creation involves users.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.C',4,'A peer-review activity helps a designer by providing:','["payment", "critique from fellow designers", "free hardware", "legal advice"]'::jsonb,1,'Peers give constructive feedback.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.D',3,'Comparing a site''s navigation test results with those of competitors helps a team to:','["ignore users", "copy their logos", "see where their design is stronger or weaker", "stop testing"]'::jsonb,2,'Benchmarking informs improvement.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.D',4,'A navigation test asks users to find an item, and the designer records the time and number of:','["fonts", "emails", "pixels", "wrong clicks"]'::jsonb,3,'Errors reveal friction.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.E',3,'Including the designer''s own voice in documentation means:','["explaining reasoning in the designer''s own words", "copying other authors", "hiding the source", "using no sources"]'::jsonb,0,'Voice shows understanding.','original'),
('127.691.d.6.E',4,'Best practices for references in a design report include:','["using only rumors", "listing sources so readers can find them", "hiding authors", "omitting sources"]'::jsonb,1,'References support credibility.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.A',3,'A usability test plan should list goals, tasks, participants and:','["the cafeteria menu", "staff salaries", "the success measures", "unrelated news"]'::jsonb,2,'Plans define how success is measured.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.A',4,'A plan that tests cognitive, perceptual, emotional and physical factors makes sure the test looks at:','["only price", "only server speed", "only the color red", "how thinking, seeing, feeling and handling affect use"]'::jsonb,3,'These dimensions cover human factors.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.B',3,'When running a usability test, the moderator should avoid:','["helping or hinting while the user works", "recording sessions", "taking notes", "giving instructions"]'::jsonb,0,'Hints bias results.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.B',4,'Collecting data during testing includes task completion rates, time on task and:','["unrelated stats", "user comments", "none", "staff opinions only"]'::jsonb,1,'Mix of numbers and comments.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.C',3,'Conclusions from a usability test should be presented with recommendations that apply:','["random ideas", "unrelated trends", "design principles and are backed by data", "personal guesses"]'::jsonb,2,'Evidence plus principles.','original'),
('127.691.d.7.C',4,'Clear communication of test results to stakeholders makes it more likely that the team will:','["cancel testing", "ignore the findings", "delay the project", "act on the recommendations"]'::jsonb,3,'Clarity drives action.','original')
) as v(code, diff, prompt, choices, ans, expl, src)
join public.teks_items i on i.code = v.code and i.is_standard
join public.teks_documents d on d.id = i.document_id and d.subject = 'Career Development' and d.language = 'en'
on conflict (fingerprint) do nothing;
